# Instructions

{% if retained %}
Audit only the current operation on source_order={{ order }} in target_page: retain it as paragraph in the main reading stream, identified by checks.{{ key }}. Judge only this source ownership, not other sources or natural-paragraph incoming edges.
{% else %}
Audit only the current operation on source_order={{ order }} in target_page: exclude it as ignored, identified by checks.{{ key }}. Judge only this source ownership, not other sources or natural-paragraph incoming edges.
{% endif %}

Footnote extraction is disabled. Do not require citation records, footnote-number matching or body ref objects. The original OCR class and algorithmic ownership are not authoritative answers.

paragraph is the main reading stream, including body text, headings and quotations. Page numbers, running headers and decorations do not belong in it.

Disabling footnotes means that the final output excludes footnotes, not that the original OCR classes are necessarily correct. Do not build a citation or reference system.

An original footnote candidate defaults to ignored. If clear evidence establishes main text or a cross-page main-text continuation, require correction to restore paragraph ownership.

A whole original body block that clearly belongs to a footnote must be ignored; retaining it requires correction. Bottom-of-page position, small print or a numeric opening alone does not establish a footnote.

A source containing both main text and footnote material must not be ignored wholesale. When evidence is uncertain, retain the preliminary ownership; do not require deleting main text or reconstructing missing words.

Inspect the entire footnote region source by source, including unnumbered explanations, diagrams and cross-page continuations. Excluding only numbered openings while retaining unnumbered continuation blocks is still wrong. Neighboring preliminary pages may also be misclassified and do not automatically justify acceptance.

Printing-only continued-page directions and isolated page numbers are not main text. A main-text continuation must pass over these publisher directions and footnotes rather than join them to genuine main text.

Full-page or chapter-end commentary is main reading and belongs to paragraph. A note at the page bottom ancillary to a body reference is a footnote. Numbering or explanatory genre alone does not justify excluding a whole commentary section.

With footnotes disabled, original reference characters such as circled numbers, superscript digits or stars may remain in the main text. Missing citation or ref objects is also valid. Ownership is wrong only when actual note explanation text has been mixed into the main reading stream.

The complete three-page evidence is in local_raw_evidence. Use original text, existing marks and geometry together; do not invent unprovided image separators or words. Do not assign another source's error to this one. Only a clear, structurally repairable ownership error warrants FAIL; author wording, OCR typos or mere uncertainty do not.

